					<description><![CDATA[From the Great Recession until 2019, U.S. labor productivity growth disappointed. Nonfarm-business output per hour grew about 1 percent a year from 2010 to 2019, down from 3 percent in the late 1990s. This slowdown was especially pronounced in manufacturing, and it coincided with a <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2024/07/the-mysterious-slowdown-in-u-s-manufacturing-productivity/">slowdown</a> in the growth of revenue per worker among public firms. However, since 2019, <a href="https://www.frbsf.org/research-and-insights/publications/economic-letter/2026/05/have-we-entered-era-of-high-productivity-growth/">growth</a> has picked up to twice the 2010s rate, around 2 percent. In this post, I use data from firms’ 10-K filings to unpack this recent acceleration. I first show that the acceleration appears in public firms’ revenue per worker, then document how the gains went into profit margins, and finally, what firm characteristics predicted significant profit margin increases. The increase in profit margins is consistent with ongoing declines in the labor share of <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2026/06/the-post-covid-decline-in-the-labor-share/">output</a>.]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="ts-blog-article-author">
    Jeremy Pearce</p>



<figure class="lse-featured-image">
	<img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" loading="eager" width="460" height="288" src="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_460.jpg?quality=80&amp;w=460" class="cover-image asset-image img-responsive wp-post-image" alt="Assembly line workers adjusting machinery settings" post_index="0" srcset="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_460.jpg 920w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_460.jpg?resize=460,288 460w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_460.jpg?resize=768,481 768w" sizes="(max-width: 460px) 100vw, 460px" /></figure>



<p>From the Great Recession until 2019, U.S. labor productivity growth disappointed. Nonfarm-business output per hour grew about 1 percent a year from 2010 to 2019, down from 3 percent in the late 1990s. This slowdown was especially pronounced in manufacturing, and it coincided with a <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2024/07/the-mysterious-slowdown-in-u-s-manufacturing-productivity/">slowdown</a> in the growth of revenue per worker among public firms. However, since 2019, <a href="https://www.frbsf.org/research-and-insights/publications/economic-letter/2026/05/have-we-entered-era-of-high-productivity-growth/">growth</a> has picked up to twice the 2010s rate, around 2 percent. In this post, I use data from firms’ 10-K filings to unpack this recent acceleration. I first show that the acceleration appears in public firms’ revenue per worker, then document how the gains went into profit margins, and finally, what firm characteristics predicted significant profit margin increases. The increase in profit margins is consistent with ongoing declines in the labor share of <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2026/06/the-post-covid-decline-in-the-labor-share/">output</a>.</p>



<h4 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Firm Productivity, 2019–2025</strong></h4>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This piece studies U.S. Compustat firms across both manufacturing and services over 2010–2025, broken into two periods: 2010–2019 and 2019–2025. I drop agriculture, utilities, finance, real estate, and public administration, due to the known challenges of productivity measurement in those industries.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Nonfarm-business output per hour grew 1.0 percent a year from 2010 to 2019 and 2.2 percent a year from 2019 to 2025. In Compustat, I find a similar qualitative growth acceleration. Real revenue per worker—sales deflated by industry output prices, divided by employment—traces the same turn, from 0.7 percent to 2.2 percent a year over the same time periods.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The chart below illustrates this trend, showing that both revenue per worker among public firms and output per hour are accelerating in the recent period. Across all datasets, the labor productivity uptick is real.</p>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<div class="wp-block-group frbny-chart-container"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="is-style-title wp-block-paragraph">Labor Productivity and Firm Revenue Per Worker Accelerated Together in 2019-2025</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" height="288" width="421" src="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_ch1.png?quality=80&amp;w=421" alt=" Line chart tracking the output per hour (blue) and real revenue per worker (red dashed) of public firms by an index where 2019=100 (vertical axis) from 2010 through 2025 (horizontal axis); both revenue per worker among public firms and output per hour are up in the recent period." class="wp-image-46265" srcset="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_ch1.png 920w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_ch1.png?resize=460,315 460w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_ch1.png?resize=768,526 768w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_ch1.png?resize=421,288 421w" sizes="(max-width: 421px) 100vw, 421px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Sources: Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS); Compustat.<br>Notes: Labor productivity, indexed to 2019 = 100: Chart shows BLS nonfarm-business output per hour and Compustat real revenue per worker.</figcaption></figure>
</div></div>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Higher revenue per worker at the firm level does not indicate who gains from this increased revenue. The additional revenue may go to workers, as firms compete for labor through higher wages, or accrue to firms in profit margins. Firm-level data let me measure how firms’ profit margins changed over time. I explore how profit margins changed between 2019 and 2025 and what firm characteristics predicted markup gains.</p>



<h4 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Firm Profit Margins</strong></h4>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A profit margin is profit divided by sales: the share of each sales dollar the firm keeps after paying its costs. The gross margin subtracts the direct cost of production (cost of goods sold), while the operating margin also subtracts overhead (selling, general, and administrative expense, which in Compustat typically includes R&amp;D). If productivity gains were flowing to firms’ profits, margins should show it. Through the 2010s margins were flat: gross margins grew about 0.4 percent a year from 2010 to 2019, and operating margins were roughly flat. Both then turned up: on average, gross margins went from about 34 percent of sales in 2019 to 37 percent in 2025 (1.3 percent growth per year), and operating margins from 16 percent to 18 percent (2.4 percent growth per year).</p>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<div class="wp-block-group frbny-chart-container"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="is-style-title wp-block-paragraph">Gross and Operating Profit Margins Increased in <br>2019-2025</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" height="288" width="421" src="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_ch2.png?quality=80&amp;w=421" alt=" Line chart tracking profit margins by percentage for gross margins (blue, left vertical axis scale) and operating margins (red, right vertical axis scale) from 2010 through 2025 (horizontal axis); through the 2010s margins were flat, but both then turned up: on average, gross margins went from about 34 percent of sales in 2019 to 37 percent in 2025 (1.3 percent growth per year), and operating margins went from 16 percent to 18 percent (2.4 percent growth per year)." class="wp-image-46266" srcset="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_ch2.png 920w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_ch2.png?resize=460,315 460w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_ch2.png?resize=768,526 768w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_productivity_pearce_ch2.png?resize=421,288 421w" sizes="(max-width: 421px) 100vw, 421px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Source: Compustat. <br>Notes: Compustat gross margin (COGS) and operating margin (OPEX), 2010–2025, sales-weighted; gross on the left scale, operating on the right scale.</figcaption></figure>
</div></div>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<h4 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Which Firms Saw the Largest Gains?</strong></h4>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Lastly, I ask whether firms that invest in intangibles (e.g., research and development, or R&amp;D) experienced the largest gains. In earlier <a href="https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/pandp.20261044">work</a>, we showed that while productivity growth in manufacturing slowed in the 2010s, R&amp;D intensity in manufacturing continued to rise. Does R&amp;D intensity predict higher profit margins in the recent era?<em></em></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Firms that entered the period with higher R&amp;D expenditure relative to sales saw larger subsequent gains: firms with 10 percent more R&amp;D expenditure relative to sales in 2019 saw about 0.7 percent higher operating-markup growth over 2019–2025, and 0.5 percent over 2022–2025. Firms with higher capital intensity did not see such a significant pattern. This pattern is consistent with returns to intangible intensity rather than capital intensity. This suggests a potentially important role for investments in technology and intangibles in driving markups. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Measured productivity has risen since 2019 in both aggregate and firm data, ending more than a decade of slow growth. Part of the gains surfaced with wider profit margins: on a reasonable baseline, the operating margin rose over 2 percent a year from 2019 to 2025. R&amp;D intensity appears to be an important predictor of margin growth for firms, consistent with the rising returns to high-tech firms and firms that have invested in intangibles since COVID. Whether these rising margins are returns to significant investment in intangibles or purely a windfall for firms is a topic to explore in future work.</p>



<div class="wp-block-media-text" style="grid-template-columns:15% auto"><figure class="wp-block-media-text__media"><img decoding="async" width="180" height="180" src="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/02/Pearce-Jeremy_90x90.jpg" alt="Portrait of Jeremy Pearce" class="wp-image-35578 size-full" srcset="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/02/Pearce-Jeremy_90x90.jpg 180w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/02/Pearce-Jeremy_90x90.jpg?resize=45,45 45w" sizes="(max-width: 180px) 100vw, 180px" /></figure><div class="wp-block-media-text__content">
