		<title>Has the AI Boom Impacted the U.S. Current Account?</title>
		<link>https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2026/10/has-the-ai-boom-impacted-the-u-s-current-account/</link>
					<comments>https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2026/10/has-the-ai-boom-impacted-the-u-s-current-account/#respond</comments>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Julian di Giovanni]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence (AI)]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[International Economics]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Macroeconomics]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/?p=46101</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[The AI boom is showing up in different corners of the U.S. economy, including international trade. Imports of semiconductors, servers, and computing equipment have climbed sharply yet the U.S. current account deficit has been largely unchanged. To better understand how the AI boom has not substantially affected the trade balance, consider that the current account is dictated by the gap between domestic saving and investment spending. It turns out that aggregate saving and investment spending have not changed much during the AI boom. Aggregate investment has not risen due to weakness outside the AI sector and business saving has remained robust.]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="ts-blog-article-author">
    Julian di Giovanni</p>



<figure class="lse-featured-image">
	<img decoding="async" loading="eager" width="460" height="288" src="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_460.jpg?quality=80&amp;w=460" class="cover-image asset-image img-responsive wp-post-image" alt="photo of construction of a large sprawling data center for artificial intelligence" post_index="0" srcset="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_460.jpg 920w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_460.jpg?resize=460,288 460w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_460.jpg?resize=768,481 768w" sizes="(max-width: 460px) 100vw, 460px" /></figure>



<p>The AI boom is showing up in different corners of the U.S. economy, including international trade. Imports of semiconductors, servers, and computing equipment have climbed sharply yet the U.S. current account deficit has been largely unchanged. To better understand how the AI boom has not substantially affected the trade balance, consider that the current account is dictated by the gap between domestic saving and investment spending. It turns out that aggregate saving and investment spending have not changed much during the AI boom. Aggregate investment has not risen due to weakness outside the AI sector and business saving has remained robust.</p>



<h4 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Gross Flows versus Net Positions</strong></h4>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35053">Recent work</a> has shown that AI-related imports accounted for a nontrivial share of U.S. imports in the past few years. In fact, it reached a high of 23&nbsp;percent in 2025.&nbsp; However, a country can import a great deal of capital equipment or attract a great deal of foreign capital without necessarily running a larger external deficit, provided it saves enough to pay for the investment spending itself. This idea can be formalized by noting that the current account is a net concept, where the balance equals a country’s gross saving less its gross domestic investment. <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2025/05/why-does-the-u-s-always-run-a-trade-deficit/">A recent <em>Liberty</em> <em>Street</em> <em>Economics</em> post</a> provides an in-depth explanation linking the trade balance to the savings and investment balance via national accounting.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For the AI boom to widen the deficit, it must raise investment without a matching rise in saving. Two channels can depress private saving: a wealth effect, where equity gains lift household consumption, or a fall in business saving. Both were at work during the dot-com era, when the <a href="https://www.brookings.edu/wp-content/uploads/2005/01/2005a_bpea_blanchard.pdf">two channels moved together</a>. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The top panel of chart below plots the savings-to-GDP and investment spending-to-GDP ratios since 2018, while the bottom panel plots the current account-to-GDP ratio. The current account deficit widened over this period, from roughly 2.2&nbsp;percent of GDP on average in 2015–19 to about 3.7&nbsp;percent since 2023. Notice, however, that the widening comes almost entirely from the saving side. Investment as a share of GDP has been remarkably steady, especially since 2023 when the AI capex buildout started in earnest.</p>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<div class="wp-block-group frbny-chart-container"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="is-style-title wp-block-paragraph">Saving, Investment, and the Current Account</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="920" height="1172" src="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_ch1a-1b_a60849.png" alt=" Two line charts tracking the investment, saving, and current account balances in percentage of GDP (vertical axes) from 2018 through 2026 (horizontal axes); top chart tracks the investment spending-to-GDP (red) and savings-to-GDP (blue) ratios, bottom chart tracks the current account-to-GDP ratio (blue); the current account deficit widened since 2018, from roughly 2.2 percent of GDP on average in 2015–19 to about 3.7 percent since 2023; however, the widening comes almost entirely from the saving side." class="wp-image-46209" srcset="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_ch1a-1b_a60849.png 920w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_ch1a-1b_a60849.png?resize=460,586 460w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_ch1a-1b_a60849.png?resize=768,978 768w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_ch1a-1b_a60849.png?resize=226,288 226w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 920px) 100vw, 920px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Sources: Bureau of Economic Analysis, NIPA Tables 1.1.5 and 5.1; authors’ calculations.</figcaption></figure>
</div></div>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Investment Has Been Reallocated, Not Expanded</strong></h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Gross domestic investment averaged 21.3 percent of GDP in the five years before the pandemic. Over 2023–2026:Q1 (the period spanning the AI capex acceleration), it averaged 21.5&nbsp;percent, and over the past four quarters, 21.1&nbsp;percent. The AI boom is thus not visible in the aggregate investment rate at all. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">To better understand why the AI boom has not had a tangible impact on aggregate investment, it is useful to look at how investment has varied across sectors of the U.S. economy. As the chart below shows, the AI contribution to nonresidential investment has not been small and has been rising in recent years, but it has coincided with weakness in other sectors of the economy. For example, the surge in factory construction that followed the 2022 industrial policy legislation has faded, and investment in structures and equipment outside the technology complex has been soft. Data centers and information-processing equipment have helped take up the slack, but not to such an extent as to raise aggregate investment relative to national savings. Further, residential investment has been declining recently.</p>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<div class="wp-block-group frbny-chart-container"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="is-style-title wp-block-paragraph">Composition of Nonresidential Investment</p>


<figure class="wdg-c3-chart wdg-c3-chart--line" data-type="line" id="bb90da3545efddbbc4cca932fec2ed74">
	<div class="wdg-c3-chart__labels">
		<p class="wdg-c3-chart__label wdg-c3-chart__label--1">Percent of GDP</p>
	</div>
