<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Consumption Has Also Not Adjusted</strong></h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It is interesting to contrast how savings have adjusted during the AI boom thus far relative to the dot-com era, which is often highlighted as a period of elevated equity values. During the dot-com expansion, private saving fell by nearly 3&nbsp;percentage points of GDP between 1995 and 2000 as households spent out of rising equity wealth. Nothing comparable is happening today. Private saving has averaged 20.8&nbsp;percent of GDP since 2023, down from 21.3&nbsp;percent before the pandemic—a difference of half a percentage point, and one that can be attributed as much to the post-pandemic unwinding of accumulated savings as to anything AI-related.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Business Sector Saving Has Not Fallen</strong></h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Let’s turn to business saving, defined as revenue minus expenses excluding investment spending. The salient difference between the dot-com and AI episodes is not the scale of the investment but the cash position of the firms doing it. The dot-com buildout was concentrated in young telecom and equipment producers with thin or negative operating cash flow; as profits compressed late in the boom while dividends remained sticky, retained earnings and with them business gross saving fell. Business saving was thus a drag on private saving at precisely the moment investment was peaking. The AI buildout, by contrast, is concentrated in the most cash-generative firms in the economy, and business saving has thus far held up through the capex surge rather than eroding against it. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The chart below illustrates these points by plotting the business sector’s savings rate over time. Business saving has averaged 10.9&nbsp;percent of GDP over the past four quarters, in line with the roughly 10.5&nbsp;percent averaged before the pandemic, and above the dot-com trough of roughly 8.8&nbsp;percent of GDP in late 2000/early 2001.</p>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<div class="wp-block-group frbny-chart-container"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="is-style-title wp-block-paragraph">Business Sector Saving</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="920" height="618" src="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_ch3_74b8ca.png" alt=" Line chart plotting the business sector’s savings rate in percentage of GDP (vertical axis) from 1992 through 2026 (horizontal axis); business saving has averaged 10.9 percent of GDP over the past four quarters, in line with the roughly 10.5 percent averaged before the pandemic." class="wp-image-46146" srcset="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_ch3_74b8ca.png 920w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_ch3_74b8ca.png?resize=460,309 460w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_ch3_74b8ca.png?resize=768,516 768w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/LSE_2026_ai-current-accounts_digiovanni_ch3_74b8ca.png?resize=429,288 429w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 920px) 100vw, 920px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Sources: Federal Reserve Board, Financial Accounts of the United States, Table F.103; authors’ calculations.</figcaption></figure>
</div></div>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<h4 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Government Saving Has Moved in the Opposite Direction</strong></h4>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Government saving offers the sharpest contrast between the two episodes, and it runs in the opposite direction from private saving. During the dot-com expansion, government saving rose by nearly 5&nbsp;percentage points of GDP, swinging from a deficit of about 1&nbsp;percent of GDP in 1995 to a surplus of roughly 4&nbsp;percent in 2000 as capital-gains-driven tax receipts flooded federal coffers. That fiscal windfall offset much of the pickup in private dissaving, which is one reason the current account deficit widened by “only” 2.5&nbsp;percentage points even as households drew down saving so aggressively.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Today the fiscal position is doing the opposite work. Government saving has fallen from about –2.2&nbsp;percent of GDP in 2015–19 to about –4.0&nbsp;percent since 2023, a deterioration of roughly 1.75&nbsp;percentage points.</p>



<h4 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Decomposing the Contributions to the Current Account Balance</strong></h4>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Putting the above pieces together, the external balance can be decomposed into its three sources relative to a pre-pandemic baseline (that is, relative to 2019:Q4). Of the roughly 2.4&nbsp;percentage point widening in the saving–investment gap, government dissaving accounts for about 1.75&nbsp;percentage points, lower private saving for about half a point, and higher investment for essentially nothing. Therefore, changes in fiscal policy have been the major driver of current account dynamics, with a smaller role played by private saving, and no discernible investment channel.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Caveat and Why Things Could Change</strong></h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There is an important caveat to note before drawing conclusions on the AI’s impact on the current account going forward. The announced capital spending plans of the major technology firms for 2026 and 2027 are considerably larger than what has already been built, while the offsetting weakness in residential and nontechnology investment will not persist indefinitely. So, while the AI boom has not yet been large enough relative to the rest of the economy to move the U.S. external balance, if the current drags on other investment fade as AI capex accelerates, both the higher investment spending and less business saving will work to increase the current account deficit.</p>



