		<title>Stablecoins and (Non)Crypto Shocks: A 2026 Update</title>
		<link>https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2026/07/stablecoins-and-noncrypto-shocks-a-2026-update/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Fri, 31 Jul 2026 11:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Cryptocurrencies]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/?p=44264</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[Stablecoins are digital assets whose value is pegged to that of a fiat currency, typically the U.S. dollar at a peg of $1.00 per token.  In a previous <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2025/04/stablecoins-and-crypto-shocks-an-update/">blog post</a>, we described the rapid growth of stablecoins through early 2025, highlighted changes in stablecoins’ reserve-asset composition, and examined their reactions to Bitcoin price shocks. In this post, we document the growth of stablecoins since our last post. Then, we examine how shocks from outside the crypto industry can impact the composition of stablecoins’ reserve assets. For our case study, we use the 2023 failure of Silicon Valley Bank (SVB) and its impact on USD Coin (USDC, issued by Circle), the second-largest stablecoin by market capitalization.]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="ts-blog-article-author">
    Kenechukwu Anadu, Pablo D. Azar, Sean Baker, Marco Cipriani, Thomas M. Eisenbach, Mattia Landoni, Gabriele La Spada, Marco Macchiavelli, and J. Christina Wang</p>



<figure class="lse-featured-image">
	<img decoding="async" loading="eager" width="460" height="288" src="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_460_ed8f02.jpg?quality=80&amp;w=460" class="cover-image asset-image img-responsive wp-post-image" alt="decorative image of bar chart and circle with stablecoin written on dark background." post_index="0" srcset="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_460_ed8f02.jpg 920w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_460_ed8f02.jpg?resize=460,288 460w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_460_ed8f02.jpg?resize=768,481 768w" sizes="(max-width: 460px) 100vw, 460px" /></figure>



<p>Stablecoins are digital assets whose value is pegged to that of a fiat currency, typically the U.S. dollar at a peg of $1.00 per token.  In a previous <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2025/04/stablecoins-and-crypto-shocks-an-update/">blog post</a>, we described the rapid growth of stablecoins through early 2025, highlighted changes in stablecoins’ reserve-asset composition, and examined their reactions to Bitcoin price shocks. In this post, we document the growth of stablecoins since our last post. Then, we examine how shocks from outside the crypto industry can impact the composition of stablecoins’ reserve assets. For our case study, we use the 2023 failure of Silicon Valley Bank (SVB) and its impact on USD Coin (USDC, issued by Circle), the second-largest stablecoin by market capitalization.</p>



<h4 class="wp-block-heading">Recent Growth of Stablecoins</h4>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Since our last post in April 2025, the market capitalization of U.S. dollar stablecoins has increased by $71 billion (30 percent) to about $308 billion recently, as shown in the chart below. This period coincides with the passage of the GENIUS Act in July 2025, which established the first federal regulatory framework for payment stablecoins.</p>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<div class="wp-block-group frbny-chart-container"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="is-style-title wp-block-paragraph">Stablecoin Market Capitalization</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="920" height="600" src="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-1a-b-1.png" alt="Two area charts tracking the market capitalization of stablecoins by billions of U.S. dollars (left chart, vertical axis) and percentage of assets (right chart, vertical axis) from 2019 through 2025 (horizontal axes) for Tether (dark gold), USD Coin (light gold), and other stablecoins (blue); the market capitalization of U.S. dollar stablecoins has increased by $71 billion to about $308 billion recently, coinciding with the passage of the GENIUS Act in July 2025." class="wp-image-44267" srcset="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-1a-b-1.png 920w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-1a-b-1.png?resize=460,300 460w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-1a-b-1.png?resize=768,501 768w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-1a-b-1.png?resize=442,288 442w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 920px) 100vw, 920px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Source: CoinGecko.<br>Note: The vertical lines indicate, respectively, SVB’s failure in March 2023 and the passage of the GENIUS Act in July 2025.</figcaption></figure>
</div></div>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The stablecoin industry remains highly concentrated, with the two largest issuers, Tether (USDT) and USDC, accounting for over 80 percent of industry assets. The reserve composition of these stablecoins differs significantly: USDC’s <a href="https://6778953.fs1.hubspotusercontent-na1.net/hubfs/6778953/USDCAttestationReports/2025/2025%20USDC_Examination%20Report%20December%2025.pdf">attested</a> reserves consist primarily of cash and short-term U.S. government securities. In contrast, corporate bonds, gold bars, Bitcoin, secured loans, and “other investments” accounted for nearly 24 percent of USDT’s <a href="https://assets.ctfassets.net/vyse88cgwfbl/20d2BoOAd28ZfkiQPYPjGN/4ed12f5939e1e06ee5aceccad4effbe4/ISAE_3000R_-_Opinion_Tether_International_Financial_Figure_31-12-2025.pdf">attested</a> reserves as of December 2025.</p>



<h4 class="wp-block-heading"><a></a>Reserve-Asset Composition and External Shocks</h4>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In prior posts, we examined the impact of <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2025/04/stablecoins-and-crypto-shocks-an-update/">positive</a> and <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2024/03/stablecoins-and-crypto-shocks/">negative</a> crypto price shocks on net flows into stablecoins. Here we study a complementary question: how do <em>non-crypto</em> shocks affect net flows into stablecoins and the composition of their reserve assets? This question is important given stablecoins’ increasing interconnectedness with traditional financial markets. To answer it, we document the changes in USDC’s reserve assets following SVB’s failure in March 2023.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">On March 11, 2023, Circle <a href="https://x.com/circle/status/1634391505988206592?s=20">reported</a> that approximately 8 percent of USDC’s reserves were held at SVB, which had been placed into FDIC receivership the previous day. Following Circle’s announcement, USDC’s secondary market price dropped considerably below $1.00, and it experienced notable <a href="https://www.bostonfed.org/publications/research-department-working-paper/2023/runs-and-flights-to-safety-are-stablecoins-the-new-money-market-funds.aspx">net outflows</a>. In addition to these effects, the composition of the <a href="https://www.blackrock.com/cash/en-us/products/329365/circle-reserve-fund">Circle Reserve Fund (CRF)</a>, a money market mutual fund (MMF) whose shares can only be purchased by Circle, changed significantly.&nbsp; (The CRF accounts for approximately 86 percent of USDC’s reserve assets as of March 2026.) &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The chart below reports the weighted average maturity (WAM)—a measure of interest-rate sensitivity—for the CRF versus the median U.S. Treasury-only (TO) MMF. Prior to SVB’s collapse, the CRF’s WAM exceeded that of the median TO MMF. Following SVB’s failure, the CRF’s WAM fell below that of the median TO fund and has remained below the 5th percentile of the WAM distribution of TO MMFs. Thus, following SVB’s failure, the CRF reduced its interest-rate risk exposure, on balance.</p>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<div class="wp-block-group frbny-chart-container"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="is-style-title wp-block-paragraph">Weighted Average Maturity: Circle Reserve Fund vs. Treasury-Only MMFs</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="920" height="614" src="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-2-1.png" alt="Line and area chart tracking the weighted average maturity (WAM) in days (vertical axis) from 2022 through 2025 (horizontal axis) of median Treasury-only money market mutual funds (TO MMF, dark blue line) and Circle Reserve Fund (CRF, gold line); light blue shaded area denotes the 5th to 95th percentile range of weighted average maturity among TO MMFs; prior to SVB’s collapse in 2023, the CRF’s WAM exceeded that of the median TO MMF; following SVB’s failure, the CRF’s WAM fell below that of the median TO MMF fund and has remained below the 5th percentile." class="wp-image-44302" srcset="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-2-1.png 920w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-2-1.png?resize=460,307 460w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-2-1.png?resize=768,513 768w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-2-1.png?resize=432,288 432w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 920px) 100vw, 920px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Sources: iMoneyNet; authors’ calculations.<br>Notes: The data are at a monthly frequency. “TO MMF”: Treasury-Only MMF.  “CRF”: Circle Reserve Fund (inception date is November 2022). The light-blue shaded area denotes the 5th to the 95th percentile range of the WAM distribution among TO MMFs. The vertical line indicates when SVB failed in March 2023.</figcaption></figure>
</div></div>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Turning to portfolio composition, the charts below show that, following SVB’s collapse, the CRF’s holdings of repurchase agreements (repos) spiked from zero to over 90 percent of its net assets. This share has since retreated some but still stands at 69 percent, above the distribution of TO MMFs’ repo holdings as a share of net assets. Thus, following the SVB shock, the CRF reduced its interest-rate risk exposure and substantially restructured its counterparty risk exposure.</p>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<div class="wp-block-group frbny-chart-container"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="is-style-title wp-block-paragraph">Repo Holdings: Circle Reserve Fund vs. Treasury-Only MMFs</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="920" height="614" src="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-3-4.png" alt="Line and area chart tracking the repurchase agreements (repos) in percentage of assets (vertical axis) from 2022 through 2025 (horizontal axis) for median Treasury-only money market mutual funds (TO MMF, dark blue line) and Circle Reserve Fund (CRF, gold line); light blue shaded area denotes the 5th to 95th percentile range of weighted average maturity among TO MMFs; following SVB’s collapse, the CRF’s holdings of repurchase agreements (repos) spiked from zero to over 90 percent of its net assets." class="wp-image-44303" srcset="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-3-4.png 920w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-3-4.png?resize=460,307 460w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-3-4.png?resize=768,513 768w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-3-4.png?resize=432,288 432w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 920px) 100vw, 920px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Sources: iMoneyNet; authors’ calculations.<br>Notes: The data are at a monthly frequency. “TO MMF”: Treasury-Only MMF. “CRF”: Circle Reserve Fund (inception date is November 2022). The light-blue shaded area denotes the 5th to the 95th percentile range of repo holdings as a percentage of net assets among TO MMFs. The vertical line indicates when SVB failed in March 2023.</figcaption></figure>
</div></div>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<div class="wp-block-group frbny-chart-container"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="is-style-title wp-block-paragraph">Circle Reserve Fund’s Repo Holdings by Counterparty Type</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="920" height="600" src="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-4-2.png" alt="Area chart depicting the Circle Reserve Fund’s (CRF) repurchase agreement (repos) holdings by percentage of total repos (vertical axis) from 2023 through 2025 (horizontal axis) for of Fixed Income Clearing Corporation (FICC, dark gold), U.S. Global Systemically Important Banks (GSIBs, light gold), and foreign (light blue); the share of FICC-sponsored repos, whose ultimate counterparties are generally entities with a net demand for funding, such as hedge funds, grew rapidly and reached 77 percent by the fourth quarter of 2025." class="wp-image-44275" srcset="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-4-2.png 920w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-4-2.png?resize=460,300 460w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-4-2.png?resize=768,501 768w, https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/LSE_2026_stablecoin-update_cipriani_ch-4-2.png?resize=442,288 442w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 920px) 100vw, 920px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Sources: Crane data; authors’ calculations.<br>Notes: The data are at a quarterly frequency. &#8220;FICC&#8221;: Fixed Income Clearing Corporation.</figcaption></figure>
</div></div>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Examining the CRF&#8217;s repo counterparty composition more closely, we observe a rapid shift starting in late 2024:&nbsp; The share of Fixed Income Clearing Corporation (FICC)-sponsored repos, whose ultimate counterparties are generally entities with a net demand for funding, such as hedge funds, grew rapidly and reached 77 percent by 2025:Q4 (see the chart above).</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Next, we turn to USDC’s reserve assets held <em>outside</em> of the CRF, which have averaged 12 percent of total reserves since the start of 2023. How has the composition of these non-CRF reserves evolved since SVB’s failure? The chart below depicts the fraction of USDC’s reserves held as bank deposits. USDC’s bank deposits rose briefly after SVB’s failure but have since remained below the levels that prevailed before SVB’s failure.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Also, the <em>types</em> of banks holding USDC&#8217;s cash changed markedly, shifting from a combination of Global Systemically Important Banks (GSIBs) and non-GSIBs (including SVB and Signature Bank) in February 2023 to predominantly GSIBs by April 2023—with Circle reporting “<a href="https://6778953.fs1.hubspotusercontent-na1.net/hubfs/6778953/USDCAttestationReports/2023/2023%20USDC_Circle%20Examination%20Report%20April%202023.pdf">in excess of 90% of cash</a>” held at GSIBs. In other words, after SVB’s collapse, USDC’s bank deposits became, and have remained, concentrated in GSIBs. &nbsp;</p>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<div class="wp-block-group frbny-chart-container"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="is-style-title wp-block-paragraph">Fraction of USDC’s Reserves Held at Banks</p>


<figure class="wdg-c3-chart wdg-c3-chart--line" data-type="line" id="1f7f3d69e0f0e04ab57a4ad637b4dc4a">
	<div class="wdg-c3-chart__labels">
		<p class="wdg-c3-chart__label wdg-c3-chart__label--1">Percentage of USDC’s assets</p>
	</div>
	<script type="application/json">{"padding":{"auto":false,"right":-7,"left":23},"data":{"groups":[],"labels":false,"type":"line","order":"desc","selection":{"enabled":false,"grouped":true,"multiple":true,"draggable":true},"x":"Date","xFormat":"%m\/%d\/%Y","rows":[["Date","Percentage of USDC's assets"],["7\/31\/2022","22"],["8\/31\/2022","20"],["9\/30\/2022","19"],["10\/31\/2022","18"],["11\/30\/2022","26"],["12\/31\/2022","24"],["1\/31\/2023","21"],["2\/28\/2023","25"],["3\/31\/2023","11"],["4\/30\/2023","17"],["5\/31\/2023","14"],["6\/30\/2023","10"],["7\/31\/2023","8"],["8\/31\/2023","7"],["9\/30\/2023","5"],["10\/31\/2023","5"],["11\/30\/2023","8"],["12\/31\/2023","10"],["1\/31\/2024","12"],["2\/29\/2024","11"],["3\/31\/2024","13"],["4\/30\/2024","9"],["5\/31\/2024","10"],["6\/30\/2024","11"],["7\/31\/2024","11"],["8\/31\/2024","11"],["9\/30\/2024","11"],["10\/31\/2024","12"],["11\/30\/2024","16"],["12\/31\/2024","15"],["1\/31\/2025","11"],["2\/28\/2025","12"],["3\/31\/2025","11"],["4\/30\/2025","12"],["5\/31\/2025","13"],["6\/30\/2025","14"],["7\/31\/2025","14"],["8\/31\/2025","14"],["9\/30\/2025","13"],["10\/31\/2025","13"],["11\/30\/2025","14"],["12\/31\/2025","12"],["1\/31\/2026","15"],["2\/28\/2026","14"],["3\/31\/2026",null]]},"legend":{"show":false,"position":"bottom"},"tooltip":{"show":true,"grouped":true,"format":"m YYYY"},"axis":{"rotated":false,"x":{"show":true,"type":"timeseries","localtime":true,"tick":{"centered":false,"culling":false,"fit":true,"outer":true,"multiline":false,"multilineMax":0,"format":"%Y","values":["01\/01\/2023","01\/01\/2024","01\/01\/2025","01\/01\/2026"]},"label":{"text":"","position":"outer-center"},"format":"%Y-%m-%d"},"y":{"show":true,"inner":false,"type":"linear","inverted":false,"tick":{"centered":false,"culling":false,"values":["0","5","10","15","20","25","30"]},"padding":{"top":3,"bottom":0},"primary":"","secondary":"","label":{"text":"","position":"outer-middle"},"min":0,"max":30},"y2":{"show":false,"inner":false,"type":"linear","inverted":false,"padding":{"top":3,"bottom":0},"label":{"text":"","position":"outer-middle"}}},"chartLabel":"Percentage of USDC\u2019s assets","color":{"pattern":["#61AEE1","#B84645","#B1812C","#046C9D","#9FA1A8","#DCB56E"]},"interaction":{"enabled":true},"point":{"show":false},"grid":{"x":{"show":false,"lines":[],"type":"indexed","stroke":""},"y":{"show":true,"lines":[],"type":"linear","stroke":""}},"regions":[],"zoom":false,"subchart":false,"download":true,"downloadText":"Download chart","downloadName":"chart","trend":{"show":false,"label":"Trend"}}</script>
