<h4 class="wp-block-heading">Who Is Buying What?</h4>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">We first exploit our consumption data to examine the types of goods that contributed the most to the divergent patterns across income groups. In the chart below, we present real cumulative spending growth for two types of goods: necessities (food and gas, top panel) and luxuries (retail spending less autos, food and gas, bottom panel). We see that real spending on luxuries increased cumulatively since 2023 for all three income groups and spending on necessities declined for most groups. Notably, the growth in retail spending has been driven by the growth in luxury spending. We also see that growth of both necessity and luxury spending by income group displayed the same K‑shaped pattern as seen in total retail spending.</p>



<div style="height:15px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<p class="is-style-title wp-block-paragraph">K-Shaped Pattern Evident for Both Necessity and Luxury Spending</p>



