		<title>A Closer Look at Emerging Market Resilience During Recent Shocks</title>
		<link>https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2026/04/a-closer-look-at-emerging-market-resilience-during-recent-shocks/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 09 Apr 2026 11:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[International Economics]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/?p=41027</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[A succession of shocks to the global economy in recent years has focused <a href="https://www.imf.org/-/media/files/publications/weo/2025/october/english/ch2.pdf">attention</a> on the improved economic and financial resilience of emerging market economies. For some of these economies, this assessment is well-founded and highlights the fruits of deep, structural economic reforms since the 1990s. However, for a much larger universe of countries, the ability to weather shocks is still mixed and many remain vulnerable. In this post, we explore the divide between the two sets of countries and focus on the effects of recent economic shocks, including the ongoing conflict in the Middle East.]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="ts-blog-article-author">
    Hunter L. Clark, Jeffrey B. Dawson, and Julian Gonzalez-Murphy</p>



<figure class="lse-featured-image">
